WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:06.910
Some work doesn't fit a single request and response.

2
00:00:07.010 --> 00:00:09.070
A long job that fans out into many

3
00:00:09.070 --> 00:00:11.510
— done only when all of them finish.

4
00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:12.720
Do it the naive way,

5
00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:15.570
and a crash halfway through strands everything.

6
00:00:15.670 --> 00:00:17.320
Chenile declares it as data,

7
00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:20.340
generates the plumbing, and survives the crash.

8
00:00:20.990 --> 00:00:24.180
The idea is to split the work into two kinds of thing.

9
00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:25.840
A brain that owns the state

10
00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:28.390
— what happens next, and never loses it.

11
00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:30.690
And a muscle that does the actual work.

12
00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:33.370
Stateless, and you run as many as you like.

13
00:00:33.470 --> 00:00:35.820
In p1 that's two small deployables.

14
00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:37.620
One server, one worker.

15
00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:40.360
Between them, one Kafka topic and a callback.

16
00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:42.130
That's the whole coupling.

17
00:00:42.230 --> 00:00:45.250
And the flow itself is declared once, as data.

18
00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:47.160
Here's p1 as data:

19
00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:48.880
a batch, and an item.

20
00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:51.130
Leaf or not decides the worker.

21
00:00:51.130 --> 00:00:54.840
A leaf executes; a non-leaf splits and aggregates.

22
00:00:54.940 --> 00:00:58.450
Typed fields become typed input and output objects.

23
00:00:58.550 --> 00:01:01.870
Each definition names the topic its work rides on.

24
00:01:01.970 --> 00:01:03.680
Schedules live in their own file

25
00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:05.850
— seeded off until you turn them on.

26
00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:08.520
One JGen blueprint turns those

27
00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:11.190
two files into a running deployment.

28
00:01:11.290 --> 00:01:13.410
It composes smaller blueprints

29
00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:16.830
— a server, a worker, one JAR per definition.

30
00:01:16.930 --> 00:01:20.470
The server holds the state and ships no business logic.

31
00:01:20.570 --> 00:01:23.710
The worker is a headless Chenile listener on Kafka

32
00:01:23.710 --> 00:01:25.150
— itself generated.

33
00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:29.250
And one worker module per process type, waiting for your logic.

34
00:01:29.350 --> 00:01:32.390
Everything but the domain logic is generated.

35
00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:35.830
The splitter turns one batch into many children.

36
00:01:35.930 --> 00:01:38.110
For each item, make a typed payload.

37
00:01:38.110 --> 00:01:40.530
The base class reports back for you.

38
00:01:40.630 --> 00:01:42.200
The executor is the leaf

39
00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:44.740
— typed input in, typed output out.

40
00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:46.120
Do the real work here.

41
00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:48.570
Throw, and it's recorded as a failure.

42
00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:51.480
The aggregator folds the children back together.

43
00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:54.770
Chenile ships the result type, so you don't invent one.

44
00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:55.740
Three methods.

45
00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:58.300
No dispatch, no retries, no plumbing.

46
00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:00.320
Watch one batch run.

47
00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:03.130
A trigger commits durably and returns 202

48
00:02:03.130 --> 00:02:04.960
— before any work runs.

49
00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:06.400
It replays as a create.

50
00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:08.680
The batch enters the state machine.

51
00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:11.050
Its next step is written to the outbox,

52
00:02:11.050 --> 00:02:13.450
in the same transaction as the state.

53
00:02:13.550 --> 00:02:17.510
A poller publishes the work to Kafka, carrying the tenant along.

54
00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:20.440
The worker consumes it and routes to your splitter.

55
00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:21.730
The splitter calls back

56
00:02:21.730 --> 00:02:24.140
— finding the server through the registry.

57
00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:28.440
Three items are created, each executed over the very same loop.

58
00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:29.960
Then the batch aggregates,

59
00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:32.400
reaches processed, and emits completion.

60
00:02:33.050 --> 00:02:35.350
Kafka can deliver a message twice.

61
00:02:35.450 --> 00:02:38.320
Three guards turn that into exactly-once effects.

62
00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:40.660
A replayed child is counted once; a

63
00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:43.410
replayed completion makes no duplicate.

64
00:02:43.510 --> 00:02:44.600
Crash the worker.

65
00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:45.560
Nothing is lost

66
00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:47.340
— the work just resumes.

67
00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:50.980
State and its consequences commit together, or not at all.

68
00:02:51.630 --> 00:02:53.530
Start the server, then the worker.

69
00:02:53.530 --> 00:02:55.680
The worker joins a consumer group.

70
00:02:55.780 --> 00:02:56.720
Need throughput?

71
00:02:56.720 --> 00:02:57.720
Run more workers.

72
00:02:57.720 --> 00:02:59.550
The broker shares the load.

73
00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:01.610
Kick off a batch with one call.

74
00:03:01.710 --> 00:03:02.760
Watch the tree fill

75
00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:05.010
— a batch, its items, turning green.

76
00:03:05.110 --> 00:03:06.970
Every causal edge is queryable,

77
00:03:06.970 --> 00:03:09.330
and you can watch the outbox drain.

78
00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:12.020
A brain, a muscle, one topic.

79
00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:14.880
Declared as data, durable by default.

80
00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:18.470
The full tutorial and the p1 sample are linked below.

81
00:03:18.570 --> 00:03:19.870
Go build one.
